::ENG1_U06_UA_Q01::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q01
Scenario: A student is preparing a presentation about source evaluation. Which choice best shows a clear perspective and coherent focus? {
=A. The presentation states one clear point about source evaluation and uses details that support that point.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
~B. The presentation lists several unrelated school topics.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~C. The presentation uses a decorative image with no explanation.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~D. The presentation avoids stating what the audience should understand.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q02::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q02
Slide note: A student wants to support a presentation about revision clarity. Which slide note is most audience-ready? {
~A. Lots of stuff about revision clarity and other things.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~B. A copied paragraph with every source detail on one slide.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
=C. Concise evidence connects revision clarity to the presentation perspective.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
~D. An unrelated sentence that does not support the message.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q03::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q03
Speaker note: A slide gives a short point about peer feedback. Which speaker note best explains rather than repeats the slide? {
~A. I will read the slide exactly as written.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~B. This slide is here because it is on the screen.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~C. Now I will talk about a different topic.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
=D. This point matters because it helps the audience understand how peer feedback supports the overall message.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q04::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q04
Revision task: Which change would most improve clarity for a presentation about  {
=A. Replace vague wording with precise language that names the communication purpose.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
~B. Add more unrelated facts to make the slide longer.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~C. Remove the perspective so the audience can guess the point.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~D. Use smaller text so a paragraph fits on the slide.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q05::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q05
Readiness check: Which final check best prepares a presentation about research evidence for an audience? {
~A. Check only whether the slides have colors.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
=B. Confirm that slides, speaker notes, evidence, and wording support the same audience-focused message.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
~C. Add new unrelated content at the end.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~D. Ignore conventions and source reliability.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q06::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q06
Scenario: A student is preparing a presentation about study groups. Which choice best shows a clear perspective and coherent focus? {
~A. The presentation lists several unrelated school topics.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
=B. The presentation states one clear point about study groups and uses details that support that point.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
~C. The presentation uses a decorative image with no explanation.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~D. The presentation avoids stating what the audience should understand.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q07::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q07
Slide note: A student wants to support a presentation about presentation readiness. Which slide note is most audience-ready? {
=A. Concise evidence connects presentation readiness to the presentation perspective.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
~B. Lots of stuff about presentation readiness and other things.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~C. A copied paragraph with every source detail on one slide.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~D. An unrelated sentence that does not support the message.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q08::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q08
Speaker note: A slide gives a short point about digital slides. Which speaker note best explains rather than repeats the slide? {
~A. I will read the slide exactly as written.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~B. This slide is here because it is on the screen.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~C. Now I will talk about a different topic.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
=D. This point matters because it helps the audience understand how digital slides supports the overall message.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q09::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q09
Claim: Students should use a planner to manage assignments. Which evidence best supports the claim in an oral presentation? {
~A. Some planners have colorful covers.#Incorrect. This detail may be true, but it does not show how planners help manage assignments.
~B. Many stores sell planners in August.#Incorrect. This fact does not explain why planners help students manage assignments.
=C. A teacher reports that students who write due dates down are more likely to submit long-term assignments on time.#Correct. This evidence connects directly to assignment management and comes from a relevant school source.
~D. A student says notebooks are heavy.#Incorrect. This does not support the claim about using planners to manage assignments.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q10::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q10
Readiness check: Which final check best prepares a presentation about audience awareness for an audience? {
=A. Confirm that slides, speaker notes, evidence, and wording support the same audience-focused message.#Correct. This choice applies the Unit 6 communication skill accurately and keeps the presentation focused for the audience.
~B. Check only whether the slides have colors.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~C. Add new unrelated content at the end.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
~D. Ignore conventions and source reliability.#Incorrect. This choice weakens the communication task because it is vague, unsupported, unrelated, or not audience-ready.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q11::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q11
Digital presentation purpose: Explain how weekly planning helps students manage assignments. Which slide should come first? {
~A. A final thank-you slide#Incorrect. A thank-you slide belongs at the end, not before the audience knows the perspective.
~B. A random image of a backpack#Incorrect. An image without purpose does not establish coherent ideas.
=C. A slide stating the topic and clear perspective#Correct. A digital presentation should begin by orienting the audience to the main idea.
~D. A list of unrelated homework complaints#Incorrect. This does not introduce a clear presentation focus.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q12::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q12
Perspective: Source checks help students choose stronger research evidence. Which slide note is clearest? {
~A. Stuff about sources and research#Incorrect. This wording is vague and would not help the audience understand the evidence.
=B. Check author, date, and purpose before using a source.#Correct. This slide note is concise, clear, and connected to the presentation perspective.
~C. Sources can be on websites and in other places.#Incorrect. This is broad and does not explain the source-checking strategy.
~D. Research is something students do in school.#Incorrect. This is too general and does not support the specific perspective.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q13::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q13
Slide note: Revision improves clarity. Which speaker note best supports the slide? {
~A. Revision improves clarity because the slide says it does.#Incorrect. This only repeats the slide without adding explanation.
~B. Revision is a word on this slide.#Incorrect. This does not explain the meaning of the slide.
~C. I will read the slide now.#Incorrect. Reading the slide aloud does not add meaningful oral explanation.
=D. Revision helps writers make ideas easier to follow by improving word choice, transitions, and explanations.#Correct. This speaker note explains the slide instead of simply repeating it.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q14::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q14
Scenario: A student will speak to classmates about why everyone should bring a notebook to advisory. What is the speaker's main purpose? {
~A. To entertain classmates with a funny story#Incorrect. The scenario does not focus on humor or storytelling; it asks the speaker to explain why classmates should do something.
~B. To summarize a long article about notebooks#Incorrect. No article is being summarized, and the task is about preparing an oral message.
~C. To compare several school supplies#Incorrect. The scenario names one action, bringing a notebook, rather than comparing multiple supplies.
=D. To encourage classmates to bring a notebook#Correct. The speaker is trying to influence classmates to take a specific action, so this identifies the purpose.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q15::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q15
Scenario: A student is preparing a short presentation for school administrators about adding more quiet study space after school. Which language choice best fits the audience? {
=A. Additional quiet study space could support students who need focused time before leaving campus.#Correct. This wording is formal, specific, and appropriate for administrators.
~B. Quiet rooms are awesome and everybody knows it.#Incorrect. This is too casual and unsupported for school administrators.
~C. We need more places because students are tired.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not clearly fit a formal school audience.
~D. People should just let us study somewhere.#Incorrect. This sounds informal and demanding instead of audience-aware.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q16::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q16
Scenario: A speaker wants to use reliable evidence about online research habits. Which source is most credible? {
~A. A random comment that says research is annoying#Incorrect. A random comment is not a reliable or valid source for a presentation.
=B. A school survey that reports how students evaluate websites for assignments#Correct. A school survey is relevant and provides evidence connected to the topic.
~C. A meme about students copying information#Incorrect. A meme is not strong evidence for an academic oral presentation.
~D. A friend's guess about how many students check sources#Incorrect. A guess is not reliable evidence because it is not based on clear data.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q17::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q17
Topic: classroom discussion. Which sentence gives the clearest oral presentation perspective? {
~A. Classroom discussion is the topic.#Incorrect. This only names the topic and does not give a focused perspective.
~B. Classrooms have discussion sometimes.#Incorrect. This is a vague detail, not a perspective.
=C. Classroom discussion helps students understand texts because they hear and explain different interpretations.#Correct. This gives a clear point of view and explains the focus.
~D. I will talk about discussion for a while.#Incorrect. This names the activity but does not state the speaker's point.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q18::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q18
Presentation purpose: Show a problem and then recommend a solution. Which structure fits best? {
~A. Compare-contrast#Incorrect. Compare-contrast is for similarities and differences, not mainly problem and solution.
~B. Random order#Incorrect. Random order weakens coherence.
=C. Problem-solution#Correct. This structure matches a presentation that identifies a problem and recommends a solution.
~D. Alphabetical order#Incorrect. Alphabetical order usually does not build meaning for this purpose.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q19::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q19
Which evidence source is most credible for a digital presentation about student research habits? {
~A. A random anonymous comment online#Incorrect. Anonymous comments are difficult to verify and are weak evidence for an academic presentation.
=B. A school survey showing how students choose online sources#Correct. A school survey is relevant and provides evidence connected to the presentation topic.
~C. A friend's guess about research habits#Incorrect. A guess is not reliable enough to support a digital presentation.
~D. A joke about students searching the internet#Incorrect. A joke is not reliable research evidence.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q20::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q20
Which slide note is best for an integrated oral and digital presentation? {
=A. Feedback reveals unclear claims.#Correct. Review the lesson explanation to confirm why this choice fits or does not fit.
~B. A long paragraph with every planned sentence#Incorrect. Slides should not carry the whole speech.
~C. Stuff about feedback and writing#Incorrect. This is too vague to guide the audience.
~D. I will say something here.#Incorrect. This does not communicate a useful idea.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q21::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q21
Scenario: Topic: school clubs. Audience: ninth-grade students. Which sentence states the clearest perspective? {
~A. This presentation is about school clubs.#Incorrect. This only names the topic and does not give a clear perspective.
~B. There are many different school clubs.#Incorrect. This is a fact-like statement, but it does not show the speaker's point of view.
~C. School clubs happen after school on different days.#Incorrect. This gives a detail, not a focused perspective.
=D. Joining a school club can help ninth-grade students meet people and feel connected to school.#Correct. This is a focused point of view that can guide a presentation.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q22::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q22
Scenario: A speaker is addressing parents about student organization habits. Which phrase uses academic vocabulary appropriately? {
~A. Staying on top of stuff#Incorrect. This phrase is conversational and vague, so it does not fit academic speaking well.
~B. Doing things better#Incorrect. This phrase is too general and does not clearly communicate the idea.
=C. Developing consistent organization routines#Correct. This phrase uses precise academic vocabulary appropriate for a formal audience.
~D. Getting it together#Incorrect. This is informal and would weaken a prepared oral presentation.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q23::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q23
Which order is strongest for presenting oral information with evidence? {
=A. Claim, reason, evidence, explanation#Correct. This order helps listeners understand the point before hearing and interpreting the evidence.
~B. Evidence, unrelated detail, claim, joke#Incorrect. This order is confusing and includes information that does not support the claim.
~C. Source title, source date, closing sentence, claim#Incorrect. Source information alone does not create a clear oral sequence.
~D. Explanation, new topic, evidence, claim#Incorrect. This order delays the claim and interrupts the focus with a new topic.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q24::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q24
Perspective: Weekly planning helps students manage long-term assignments. Which detail best supports the perspective? {
~A. Some planners have spiral binding.#Incorrect. This detail is about a planner's design, not how planning helps students manage assignments.
~B. Many students carry backpacks.#Incorrect. This may be school-related, but it does not support the planning perspective.
~C. Students sometimes talk in class.#Incorrect. This is unrelated to weekly planning and assignment management.
=D. Long-term assignments can be divided into smaller tasks across several days.#Correct. This detail supports the perspective by showing how planning helps.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q25::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q25
Which slide sequence is most coherent? {
~A. Funny image, thank-you slide, problem, title#Incorrect. This order confuses the audience and delays the focus.
=B. Title and perspective, problem, solution, final takeaway#Correct. This sequence builds a clear idea in logical order.
~C. Solution, unrelated quote, title, random fact#Incorrect. This sequence does not develop one coherent idea.
~D. Final takeaway, title, unrelated image, problem#Incorrect. The final takeaway should not appear before the audience understands the presentation.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q26::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q26
Weak slide note: The research source thing is helpful because people can see whether source stuff is good. Which revision is clearest? {
~A. People can look at the thing and know stuff.#Incorrect. This wording is still unclear and informal.
~B. Source stuff is good for many things.#Incorrect. This remains vague and does not improve clarity.
~C. Research is research when people research it.#Incorrect. This is circular and does not explain the idea.
=D. Source checks help students judge whether evidence is reliable.#Correct. This revision is concise, clear, and uses precise language.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q27::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q27
In a presentation, the word "credible" means which of the following? {
=A. trustworthy and reliable#Correct. In this context, credible evidence is evidence the audience can trust.
~B. confusing and unsupported#Incorrect. Credible information should be clear and supported.
~C. colorful and decorative#Incorrect. Color and decoration do not determine credibility.
~D. unrelated but interesting#Incorrect. Interesting information is not necessarily credible or relevant.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q28::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q28
Scenario: A student plans to explain recycling rules to new students. Which planning note best includes purpose, audience, and perspective? {
~A. Recycling rules are posted near the cafeteria.#Incorrect. This is only a detail and does not include the full speaking plan.
~B. New students need to hear a presentation in the cafeteria.#Incorrect. This names an audience and place but does not state a focused perspective.
=C. I will explain to new students how following recycling rules keeps shared spaces cleaner.#Correct. This note includes purpose, audience, and a clear perspective.
~D. My topic is recycling at school.#Incorrect. This only names the topic and leaves the purpose and perspective unclear.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q29::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q29
Scenario: A student will speak to classmates about using a homework checklist. What is the clearest purpose? {
~A. To describe every assignment from the week#Incorrect. That would list information, but the scenario focuses on a checklist habit.
=B. To explain how a checklist can help classmates manage homework#Correct. This purpose fits the audience and explains what the speaker wants listeners to understand.
~C. To entertain classmates with a story about homework#Incorrect. Nothing in the scenario suggests the purpose is entertainment.
~D. To compare school rules from different classes#Incorrect. The task is about a homework checklist, not comparing rules.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q30::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q30
Claim: Quiet study spaces help students complete work. Which explanation best connects evidence to the claim? Evidence: The library sign-in sheet shows more student visits during project weeks. {
~A. The library has tables and chairs.#Incorrect. This describes the place but does not explain how the evidence supports the claim.
=B. More visits during project weeks suggest students seek quiet space when they need focused work time.#Correct. This explanation connects the evidence directly to the claim.
~C. Project weeks happen during the school year.#Incorrect. This repeats a basic detail without connecting it to student work.
~D. Students sometimes like going to the library.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not explain the specific evidence.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q31::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q31
Perspective: Students should use study groups before quizzes. Opposing view: Some students learn better alone. Which response keeps coherent focus? {
~A. Studying alone is boring, and school lunches should change.#Incorrect. This dismisses the opposing view and shifts to an unrelated topic.
~B. Study groups are always perfect for every person.#Incorrect. This is too absolute and does not fairly handle the opposing view.
~C. Quizzes are sometimes difficult.#Incorrect. This is related to school but does not respond to the opposing view.
=D. Some students do learn well alone, but study groups can still help students hear explanations they may not create by themselves.#Correct. This acknowledges the opposing view and returns to the main perspective.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q32::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q32
Which slide note best fits an expository digital presentation? {
=A. Step 1: Identify all due dates for the week.#Correct. This note clearly explains one step in an organized process.
~B. Due dates are annoying and nobody likes them.#Incorrect. This is informal and not explanatory.
~C. I do not know what the next slide is about.#Incorrect. This shows lack of organization.
~D. Look at this unrelated picture.#Incorrect. This does not explain information.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q33::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q33
Which phrase uses academic vocabulary appropriately for a slide about evidence? {
~A. pretty good source stuff#Incorrect. This phrase is informal and vague.
~B. random facts from somewhere#Incorrect. This phrase suggests weak evidence and lacks precision.
=C. credible evidence from a reliable source#Correct. This phrase uses precise academic vocabulary appropriate for the topic.
~D. things that help maybe#Incorrect. This wording is too uncertain and unclear.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q34::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q34
Which slide note uses standard English conventions? {
=A. Revision helps writers make ideas clearer.#Correct. The sentence uses correct subject-verb agreement.
~B. Revision help writers make ideas clearer.#Incorrect. The verb does not agree with the singular subject "Revision."
~C. Revision help writer's make ideas clear.#Incorrect. This has agreement and apostrophe errors.
~D. Revision helping writers make ideas clearer.#Incorrect. This is a fragment rather than a complete sentence.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q35::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q35
Scenario: Audience: school administrators. Topic: adding a tutoring table after school. Which wording best fits the audience? {
~A. We really need this because students are stressed.#Incorrect. This is understandable but too general for an administrator audience.
=B. An after-school tutoring table could provide targeted academic support for students who need help before going home.#Correct. This is formal, specific, and appropriate for administrators.
~C. Tutoring is a thing people probably want.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal, so it weakens the message.
~D. Everybody should just agree to make tutoring happen.#Incorrect. This sounds demanding and does not show audience awareness.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q36::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q36
Scenario: Topic: reading before bed. Which sentence is a clear perspective statement? {
~A. Reading before bed is the topic of my presentation.#Incorrect. This only names the topic and does not state a perspective.
~B. Many books can be read at night.#Incorrect. This is a general detail, not a guiding perspective.
~C. Some people read and some people do not.#Incorrect. This is a vague observation and does not guide a presentation.
=D. Reading before bed can help students create a calmer routine before sleep.#Correct. This sentence gives a clear point of view about the topic.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q37::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q37
Claim: Students should learn how to evaluate online information. Which reason best supports the claim? {
~A. The internet has many colors and videos.#Incorrect. This observation does not explain why evaluation skills are important.
~B. Some websites have short paragraphs.#Incorrect. Paragraph length does not prove that evaluation skills are needed.
=C. Students need to know whether online information is trustworthy before using it in assignments.#Correct. This reason logically supports the claim and prepares for credible evidence.
~D. Online information is easier to copy than textbook information.#Incorrect. This does not support responsible evaluation of sources.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q38::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q38
Which phrase uses academic vocabulary best in a focused oral presentation? {
~A. stuff I want to say#Incorrect. This phrase is informal and vague.
~B. a bunch of school things#Incorrect. This wording is too general for academic speaking.
~C. talking about whatever#Incorrect. This phrase shows lack of focus and inappropriate vocabulary.
=D. coherent presentation focus#Correct. This phrase uses precise academic vocabulary connected to the lesson skill.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q39::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.01
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q39
A digital presentation has slides about planning, cafeteria menus, sports scores, and homework. What is the main issue? {
~A. It has too few colors.#Incorrect. The main issue is not design color.
=B. It lacks coherent ideas because the slides do not support one perspective.#Correct. The topics are disconnected and do not build one clear message.
~C. It uses no paragraphs.#Incorrect. Slides should not rely on long paragraphs.
~D. It needs more unrelated slides.#Incorrect. More unrelated slides would make coherence worse.
}

::ENG1_U06_UA_Q40::
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG1.C.02
Question ID: ENG1_U06_UA_Q40
A slide has a full paragraph of evidence copied onto it. What is the best revision choice? {
~A. Add another full paragraph to balance the slide.#Incorrect. More text would make the slide harder to read.
~B. Remove all evidence from the presentation.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed; it should be presented clearly.
=C. Use a concise evidence note on the slide and explain the evidence orally.#Correct. This keeps the slide clear while preserving the support.
~D. Make the paragraph smaller so more words fit.#Incorrect. Smaller text does not solve the clarity problem.
}
